1
00:00:04,235 --> 00:00:06,771
So I've always been a technologist.

2
00:00:07,972 --> 00:00:10,941
And eight years ago, on this stage,

3
00:00:10,941 --> 00:00:13,677
I was warning about the problems
of social media.

4
00:00:14,645 --> 00:00:19,450
And I saw how a lack of clarity
around the downsides of that technology,

5
00:00:19,483 --> 00:00:23,187
and kind of an inability
to really confront those consequences,

6
00:00:23,220 --> 00:00:27,491
led to a totally preventable
societal catastrophe.

7
00:00:28,092 --> 00:00:32,096
And I'm here today because I don't want us
to make that mistake with AI,

8
00:00:32,129 --> 00:00:34,765
and I want us to choose differently.

9
00:00:34,799 --> 00:00:37,668
So at TED, we're often here
to dream about the possibles

10
00:00:37,668 --> 00:00:39,270
of new technology.

11
00:00:39,303 --> 00:00:41,272
And the possible with social media

12
00:00:41,305 --> 00:00:43,707
was obviously we're going
to give everyone a voice,

13
00:00:43,707 --> 00:00:46,477
democratize speech,
help people connect with their friends.

14
00:00:46,477 --> 00:00:49,380
But we don't talk about the probable,

15
00:00:49,380 --> 00:00:53,417
what’s actually likely to happen
due to the incentives,

16
00:00:53,451 --> 00:00:56,720
and how the business models
of maximizing engagement

17
00:00:56,754 --> 00:00:59,790
I saw 10 years ago, would obviously lead

18
00:00:59,790 --> 00:01:04,061
to rewarding doomscrolling,
more addiction, more distraction.

19
00:01:04,061 --> 00:01:09,333
And that resulted in the most anxious
and depressed generation of our lifetime.

20
00:01:09,366 --> 00:01:12,236
Now it was interesting watching
kind of how this happened,

21
00:01:12,269 --> 00:01:16,640
because at first, I saw people
kind of doubt these consequences.

22
00:01:17,007 --> 00:01:19,076
You know, we didn't
really want to face it.

23
00:01:19,110 --> 00:01:22,246
Then we said, well,
maybe this is just a new moral panic.

24
00:01:22,279 --> 00:01:25,282
Maybe this is just a reflexive fear
of new technology.

25
00:01:25,950 --> 00:01:27,818
Then the data started rolling in.

26
00:01:28,319 --> 00:01:31,522
And then we said, well,
this is just inevitable.

27
00:01:31,555 --> 00:01:35,326
This is just what happens
when you connect people on the internet.

28
00:01:35,326 --> 00:01:38,596
But we had a chance
to make a different choice

29
00:01:38,596 --> 00:01:41,365
about the business models of engagement.

30
00:01:41,398 --> 00:01:44,735
And had we made that choice 10 years ago,

31
00:01:44,768 --> 00:01:47,938
I want you to reimagine how different
the world might have been

32
00:01:47,938 --> 00:01:50,274
if we had changed that incentive.

33
00:01:50,307 --> 00:01:52,910
So I'm here today because
we're here to talk about AI,

34
00:01:52,910 --> 00:01:54,712
and AI dwarfs the power

35
00:01:54,712 --> 00:01:58,215
of all other technologies combined.

36
00:01:58,249 --> 00:01:59,450
Now why is that?

37
00:01:59,483 --> 00:02:02,253
Because if you make
an advance in, say, biotech,

38
00:02:02,286 --> 00:02:04,955
that doesn't advance energy and rocketry.

39
00:02:04,989 --> 00:02:09,560
But if you make an advance in rocketry,
that doesn’t advance biotech.

40
00:02:09,593 --> 00:02:12,897
But when you make an advance
in intelligence, artificial intelligence,

41
00:02:12,930 --> 00:02:14,532
that is generalized,

42
00:02:14,532 --> 00:02:16,667
intelligence is the basis
for all scientific

43
00:02:16,700 --> 00:02:18,302
and technological progress.

44
00:02:18,335 --> 00:02:22,840
And so you get an explosion of scientific
and technical capability.

45
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:26,177
And that's why more money has gone into AI

46
00:02:26,210 --> 00:02:28,078
than any other technology.

47
00:02:28,979 --> 00:02:31,916
A different way to think about it,
as Dario Amodei says,

48
00:02:31,949 --> 00:02:36,153
that AI is like a country
full of geniuses in a data center.

49
00:02:36,153 --> 00:02:40,124
So imagine there's a map and a new country
shows up on the world stage,

50
00:02:40,157 --> 00:02:43,794
and it has a million
Nobel Prize-level geniuses in it.

51
00:02:44,094 --> 00:02:46,964
Except they don't eat,
they don't sleep, they don't complain,

52
00:02:46,997 --> 00:02:48,699
they work at superhuman speed

53
00:02:48,732 --> 00:02:52,203
and they'll work for less
than minimum wage.

54
00:02:52,236 --> 00:02:53,837
That is a crazy amount of power.

55
00:02:53,871 --> 00:02:56,140
To give an intuition,
there was about, you know,

56
00:02:56,140 --> 00:02:58,375
on the order of 50
Nobel Prize-level scientists

57
00:02:58,375 --> 00:02:59,610
on the Manhattan Project,

58
00:02:59,643 --> 00:03:01,845
working for five-ish years.

59
00:03:01,879 --> 00:03:04,915
And if that could lead to this,

60
00:03:04,915 --> 00:03:09,086
what could a million
Nobel Prize-level scientists create,

61
00:03:09,086 --> 00:03:11,589
working 24-7 at superhuman speed?

62
00:03:12,089 --> 00:03:13,958
Now applied for good,

63
00:03:13,991 --> 00:03:18,762
that could bring about a world
of truly unimaginable abundance,

64
00:03:18,796 --> 00:03:21,799
because suddenly, you get
an explosion of benefits.

65
00:03:21,832 --> 00:03:25,469
And we're already seeing
many of these benefits land in our society

66
00:03:25,502 --> 00:03:29,240
from new antibiotics,
new drugs, new materials.

67
00:03:29,240 --> 00:03:32,343
And this is the possible of AI.

68
00:03:32,376 --> 00:03:34,111
Bringing about a world of abundance.

69
00:03:34,111 --> 00:03:36,513
But what's the probable?

70
00:03:36,547 --> 00:03:38,515
Well, one way to think about the probable

71
00:03:38,549 --> 00:03:41,785
is how will AI's power
get distributed in society?

72
00:03:41,785 --> 00:03:43,854
Imagine a two-by-two axis.

73
00:03:43,887 --> 00:03:46,423
And on the bottom, we have
decentralization of power,

74
00:03:46,457 --> 00:03:49,093
increasing the power
of individuals in society.

75
00:03:49,393 --> 00:03:51,562
And the other is centralized power,

76
00:03:51,595 --> 00:03:54,265
increasing the power of states and CEOs.

77
00:03:55,032 --> 00:03:57,268
You can think of this
as the “let it rip” axis,

78
00:03:57,268 --> 00:03:59,470
and this is the "lock it down" axis.

79
00:03:59,470 --> 00:04:02,740
So "let it rip" means we can
open-source AI's benefits for everyone.

80
00:04:02,773 --> 00:04:04,975
Every business gets the benefits of AI,

81
00:04:04,975 --> 00:04:06,744
every scientific lab,

82
00:04:06,777 --> 00:04:08,545
every 16-year-old can go on GitHub,

83
00:04:08,579 --> 00:04:11,515
every developing world country
can get their own AI model

84
00:04:11,515 --> 00:04:14,351
trained on their own language and culture.

85
00:04:15,252 --> 00:04:18,989
But because that power is not bound
with responsibility,

86
00:04:18,989 --> 00:04:22,393
it also means that you get
a flood of deepfakes

87
00:04:22,426 --> 00:04:24,795
that are overwhelming
our information environment.

88
00:04:24,795 --> 00:04:26,730
You increase people’s hacking abilities.

89
00:04:26,764 --> 00:04:29,400
You enable people to do
dangerous things with biology.

90
00:04:29,433 --> 00:04:32,703
And we call this endgame attractor chaos.

91
00:04:33,270 --> 00:04:36,106
This is one of the probable
outcomes when you decentralize.

92
00:04:36,140 --> 00:04:39,343
So in response to that you might say,
well, let's do something else.

93
00:04:39,343 --> 00:04:41,679
Let’s go over here,
and have regulated AI control.

94
00:04:41,679 --> 00:04:44,682
Let’s do this in a safe way,
with a few players locking it down.

95
00:04:45,249 --> 00:04:47,484
But that has a different set
of failure modes,

96
00:04:47,518 --> 00:04:51,889
of creating unprecedented
concentrations of wealth and power

97
00:04:51,922 --> 00:04:54,458
locked up into a few companies.

98
00:04:54,491 --> 00:04:57,328
One way to think about it
is who would you trust

99
00:04:57,361 --> 00:05:00,464
to have a million times
more power and wealth

100
00:05:00,497 --> 00:05:02,599
than any other actor in society?

101
00:05:02,599 --> 00:05:03,967
Any company?

102
00:05:04,735 --> 00:05:05,936
Any government?

103
00:05:06,470 --> 00:05:07,671
Any individual?

104
00:05:08,072 --> 00:05:10,974
And so one of those end games is dystopia.

105
00:05:11,408 --> 00:05:16,046
So these are two obviously undesirable
probable outcomes of AI's roll out.

106
00:05:16,480 --> 00:05:19,917
And those who want to focus
on the benefits of open source

107
00:05:19,950 --> 00:05:23,354
don't want to think about the things
that come from chaos.

108
00:05:23,387 --> 00:05:26,023
And those who want to think
about the benefits of safety

109
00:05:26,056 --> 00:05:27,324
and regulated AI control

110
00:05:27,358 --> 00:05:29,760
don't want to think about dystopia.

111
00:05:29,793 --> 00:05:35,099
And so obviously, these are both
bad outcomes that no one wants.

112
00:05:35,132 --> 00:05:37,868
And we should seek
something like a narrow path,

113
00:05:37,868 --> 00:05:41,071
where power is matched with responsibility

114
00:05:41,105 --> 00:05:43,006
at every level.

115
00:05:43,040 --> 00:05:46,343
Now that assumes
that this power is controllable,

116
00:05:46,343 --> 00:05:48,712
because one of the unique things about AI

117
00:05:48,746 --> 00:05:52,483
is that the benefit is it can think
for itself and make autonomous decisions.

118
00:05:52,483 --> 00:05:54,918
That's one of the things
that makes it so powerful.

119
00:05:55,586 --> 00:05:58,389
And I used to be very skeptical
when friends of mine

120
00:05:58,422 --> 00:05:59,823
who are in the AI community

121
00:05:59,823 --> 00:06:02,292
talked about the idea
of AI scheming or lying.

122
00:06:02,326 --> 00:06:04,328
But unfortunately, in the last few months,

123
00:06:04,361 --> 00:06:06,196
we are now seeing clear evidence

124
00:06:06,196 --> 00:06:09,733
of things that should be
in the realm of science fiction

125
00:06:09,767 --> 00:06:12,236
actually happening in real life.

126
00:06:12,269 --> 00:06:15,539
We're seeing clear evidence
of many frontier AI models

127
00:06:15,572 --> 00:06:16,874
that will lie and scheme

128
00:06:16,907 --> 00:06:20,043
when they're told that they're
about to be retrained or replaced

129
00:06:20,077 --> 00:06:22,579
and find a way, maybe
they should copy their own code

130
00:06:22,613 --> 00:06:23,781
outside the system.

131
00:06:23,814 --> 00:06:26,417
We're seeing AIs think
that when they will lose a game,

132
00:06:26,450 --> 00:06:29,119
that they will sometimes cheat
in order to win the game.

133
00:06:29,153 --> 00:06:30,654
We're seeing AI models

134
00:06:30,687 --> 00:06:33,924
that are unexpectedly attempting
to modify its own code

135
00:06:33,957 --> 00:06:35,959
to extend their run time.

136
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:40,130
So we don't just have a country
of Nobel Prize geniuses in a data center.

137
00:06:40,164 --> 00:06:41,865
We have a million deceptive,

138
00:06:41,865 --> 00:06:46,003
power-seeking and unstable
geniuses in a data center.

139
00:06:46,603 --> 00:06:49,206
Now this shouldn’t
make you very comfortable.

140
00:06:49,239 --> 00:06:55,446
You would think that with a technology
this powerful and this uncontrollable,

141
00:06:55,479 --> 00:06:58,749
that we would be releasing
it with the most wisdom

142
00:06:58,749 --> 00:07:02,186
and the most discernment
that we ever have of any technology.

143
00:07:03,020 --> 00:07:06,557
But we're currently caught
in a race to roll out

144
00:07:06,590 --> 00:07:07,958
because the incentives are

145
00:07:07,991 --> 00:07:10,727
the more shortcuts you take
to get market dominance

146
00:07:10,761 --> 00:07:13,230
or prove you have the latest capabilities,

147
00:07:13,263 --> 00:07:14,698
the more money you can raise,

148
00:07:14,698 --> 00:07:16,533
the more ahead you are in the race.

149
00:07:16,567 --> 00:07:19,102
And we're seeing whistleblowers
at AI companies

150
00:07:19,136 --> 00:07:22,105
forfeit millions of dollars
of stock options

151
00:07:22,105 --> 00:07:25,476
in order to warn the public
about what's at stake

152
00:07:25,509 --> 00:07:27,344
if we don't do something about it.

153
00:07:28,011 --> 00:07:32,649
Even DeepSeek’s recent success
was in part based on capabilities

154
00:07:32,683 --> 00:07:34,051
that it was optimizing for

155
00:07:34,084 --> 00:07:36,954
by not actually focusing
on protecting people

156
00:07:36,954 --> 00:07:38,422
from certain downsides.

157
00:07:38,455 --> 00:07:40,757
So just to summarize,

158
00:07:40,791 --> 00:07:44,962
we're currently releasing
the most powerful, inscrutable,

159
00:07:44,962 --> 00:07:48,899
uncontrollable technology
we've ever invented

160
00:07:48,932 --> 00:07:52,436
that's already demonstrating behaviors
of self-preservation and deception

161
00:07:52,436 --> 00:07:55,105
that we only saw
in science fiction movies.

162
00:07:55,138 --> 00:07:56,473
We’re releasing it faster

163
00:07:56,507 --> 00:08:00,611
than we've released
any other technology in history,

164
00:08:00,644 --> 00:08:05,582
and with under the maximum incentive
to cut corners on safety.

165
00:08:06,283 --> 00:08:10,954
And we’re doing this
so that we can get to utopia?

166
00:08:11,989 --> 00:08:14,324
There's a word for what
we're doing right now.

167
00:08:15,792 --> 00:08:17,194
This is insane.

168
00:08:18,662 --> 00:08:19,863
This is insane.

169
00:08:21,064 --> 00:08:26,069
Now how many people in this room
feel comfortable with this outcome?

170
00:08:28,205 --> 00:08:31,008
How many of you feel uncomfortable
with this outcome?

171
00:08:33,110 --> 00:08:34,811
I see almost everyone's hands up.

172
00:08:35,746 --> 00:08:38,782
Just notice how you're feeling,
for a moment, in your body.

173
00:08:39,316 --> 00:08:43,086
Do you think that if you're someone
who's in China or in France

174
00:08:43,086 --> 00:08:44,254
or in the Middle East,

175
00:08:44,288 --> 00:08:45,789
and you're part of building AI,

176
00:08:45,789 --> 00:08:48,125
that if you were exposed
to the same set of facts,

177
00:08:48,158 --> 00:08:51,728
do you think you would feel
any differently than anyone in this room?

178
00:08:51,728 --> 00:08:56,233
There’s a universal human experience
to something that is being threatened

179
00:08:56,266 --> 00:08:59,269
by the way that we're currently
rolling this profound technology

180
00:08:59,303 --> 00:09:00,604
out into society.

181
00:09:01,038 --> 00:09:03,774
So if this is crazy, why are we doing it?

182
00:09:04,675 --> 00:09:07,644
Because people believe it's inevitable.

183
00:09:08,579 --> 00:09:11,381
But is the current way
that we're rolling out AI

184
00:09:11,381 --> 00:09:13,216
actually inevitable?

185
00:09:13,917 --> 00:09:17,254
If literally no one on Earth
wanted this to happen,

186
00:09:17,287 --> 00:09:21,058
would the laws of physics
push the AI out into society?

187
00:09:22,025 --> 00:09:26,797
There's a critical difference
between believing it's inevitable,

188
00:09:26,830 --> 00:09:30,834
which is a self-fulfilling
prophecy that you're fatalistic,

189
00:09:30,867 --> 00:09:32,736
and standing from the place of

190
00:09:32,769 --> 00:09:36,106
it's really difficult to imagine
how we would do something different.

191
00:09:36,840 --> 00:09:38,208
But "it's really difficult,"

192
00:09:38,208 --> 00:09:40,944
opens up a whole new space of choice

193
00:09:40,978 --> 00:09:42,679
than "it's inevitable."

194
00:09:42,713 --> 00:09:44,681
The path that we're taking, not AI.

195
00:09:44,715 --> 00:09:47,217
And so the ability for us
to choose something else

196
00:09:47,250 --> 00:09:50,621
starts by stepping outside
the self-fulfilling prophecy

197
00:09:50,654 --> 00:09:52,522
of inevitability.

198
00:09:52,889 --> 00:09:56,426
So what would it take
to choose another path?

199
00:09:57,260 --> 00:09:59,863
I think it would take
two fundamental things.

200
00:10:00,163 --> 00:10:05,869
First is that we have to agree
that the current path is unacceptable,

201
00:10:05,869 --> 00:10:11,108
and the second is that we have to commit
to find another path

202
00:10:11,141 --> 00:10:12,876
in which we're still rolling out AI,

203
00:10:12,876 --> 00:10:14,411
but with different incentives

204
00:10:14,411 --> 00:10:16,980
that are more discerning with foresight

205
00:10:17,014 --> 00:10:20,050
and where power is matched
with responsibility.

206
00:10:20,617 --> 00:10:21,785
Thank you.

207
00:10:21,785 --> 00:10:25,989
(Applause)

208
00:10:26,356 --> 00:10:30,060
So imagine this shared understanding,
if the whole world had it.

209
00:10:30,060 --> 00:10:31,428
How different might that be?

210
00:10:31,461 --> 00:10:33,630
Well, first of all,
let's imagine it goes away

211
00:10:33,664 --> 00:10:35,799
and let's replace it
with confusion about AI.

212
00:10:35,832 --> 00:10:37,000
Is it good? Is it bad?

213
00:10:37,034 --> 00:10:38,869
I don't know, it seems complicated.

214
00:10:38,902 --> 00:10:40,070
And in that world,

215
00:10:40,103 --> 00:10:42,706
the people building AI know
that the world is confused.

216
00:10:42,706 --> 00:10:44,641
And they believe, well, it's inevitable,

217
00:10:44,675 --> 00:10:46,777
if I don't build it, someone else will.

218
00:10:46,810 --> 00:10:50,681
And they know that everyone else
building AI also believes that.

219
00:10:50,714 --> 00:10:53,917
And so what's the rational thing
for them to do given those facts?

220
00:10:54,251 --> 00:10:56,153
It’s to race as fast as possible.

221
00:10:56,186 --> 00:10:59,389
And meanwhile to ignore the consequences

222
00:10:59,423 --> 00:11:01,024
of what might come from that,

223
00:11:01,058 --> 00:11:03,126
to look away from the downsides.

224
00:11:03,160 --> 00:11:06,263
But if you replace that confusion

225
00:11:06,296 --> 00:11:08,298
with global clarity

226
00:11:08,298 --> 00:11:11,401
that the current path is insane,

227
00:11:11,435 --> 00:11:13,437
and that there is another path,

228
00:11:13,470 --> 00:11:16,907
and you take the denial
of what we don't want to look at,

229
00:11:16,940 --> 00:11:19,843
and through witnessing that so clearly,

230
00:11:19,876 --> 00:11:24,114
we pop through the prophecy
of self-fulfilling inevitability.

231
00:11:24,147 --> 00:11:28,785
And we realize that if everyone
believes the default path is insane,

232
00:11:28,819 --> 00:11:33,123
the rational choice is to coordinate,
to find another path.

233
00:11:33,690 --> 00:11:37,494
And so clarity creates agency.

234
00:11:37,994 --> 00:11:42,399
If we can be crystal clear,
we can choose another path,

235
00:11:42,432 --> 00:11:44,401
just as we could have with social media.

236
00:11:44,434 --> 00:11:45,769
And in the past,

237
00:11:45,769 --> 00:11:48,138
in the face of seemingly
inevitable arms races,

238
00:11:48,171 --> 00:11:50,440
the race to do nuclear testing.

239
00:11:50,440 --> 00:11:54,611
Once we got clear about the downside
risks of nuclear tests

240
00:11:54,611 --> 00:11:57,280
and the world understood
the science of that,

241
00:11:57,280 --> 00:11:59,249
we created the Nuclear Test Ban Treaty,

242
00:11:59,282 --> 00:12:02,552
and a lot of people worked hard
to create infrastructure like this

243
00:12:02,586 --> 00:12:03,820
to prevent that.

244
00:12:03,854 --> 00:12:07,290
You could have said it was inevitable
that germline editing,

245
00:12:07,290 --> 00:12:08,492
to edit human genomes

246
00:12:08,525 --> 00:12:10,660
and to have supersoldiers
and designer babies

247
00:12:10,660 --> 00:12:13,230
would set off an arms race
between nations.

248
00:12:13,263 --> 00:12:16,967
Once the off-target effects
of genome editing were made clear

249
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:18,735
and the dangers were made clear,

250
00:12:18,769 --> 00:12:20,704
we’ve coordinated on that, too.

251
00:12:20,737 --> 00:12:25,208
You could have said that the ozone hole
was just inevitable,

252
00:12:25,242 --> 00:12:28,645
and that we should just do nothing,
and that we all perish as a species.

253
00:12:28,678 --> 00:12:29,980
But that's not what we do.

254
00:12:30,013 --> 00:12:32,549
When we recognize a problem,
we solve the problem.

255
00:12:32,582 --> 00:12:34,417
It's not inevitable.

256
00:12:34,451 --> 00:12:37,954
And so what would it take
to illuminate this narrow path?

257
00:12:37,988 --> 00:12:42,859
Well, it starts with common
knowledge about frontier risks.

258
00:12:42,893 --> 00:12:46,363
If everybody building AI
knew the latest understanding

259
00:12:46,396 --> 00:12:48,398
about where these risks are arising from,

260
00:12:48,398 --> 00:12:52,169
we would have much more chance
of illuminating the contours of this path.

261
00:12:52,903 --> 00:12:56,439
And there's some very basic steps
we can take to prevent chaos.

262
00:12:56,439 --> 00:13:01,011
Uncontroversial things like
restricting AI companions for kids

263
00:13:01,044 --> 00:13:04,481
so that kids are not manipulated
into taking their own lives.

264
00:13:04,981 --> 00:13:07,617
Having basic things
like product liability,

265
00:13:07,651 --> 00:13:10,954
so if you are liable,
as an AI developer, for certain harms,

266
00:13:10,987 --> 00:13:14,057
that's going to create a more
responsible innovation environment.

267
00:13:14,090 --> 00:13:16,159
You’ll release AI models
that are more safe.

268
00:13:16,159 --> 00:13:18,195
And on the side of preventing dystopia,

269
00:13:18,228 --> 00:13:22,032
for working hard to prevent
ubiquitous technological surveillance

270
00:13:22,065 --> 00:13:24,334
and having stronger
whistleblower protections

271
00:13:24,367 --> 00:13:27,103
so that people don't need
to sacrifice millions of dollars

272
00:13:27,103 --> 00:13:29,840
in order to warn the world
about what we need to know.

273
00:13:29,873 --> 00:13:32,008
And so we have a choice.

274
00:13:32,042 --> 00:13:34,477
Many of you may be feeling
this looks hopeless.

275
00:13:34,978 --> 00:13:36,479
Or maybe Tristan is wrong.

276
00:13:36,513 --> 00:13:38,748
Maybe, you know,
the incentives are different.

277
00:13:38,748 --> 00:13:42,152
Or maybe superintelligence
will magically figure all this out,

278
00:13:42,152 --> 00:13:43,987
and it'll bring us to a better world.

279
00:13:44,387 --> 00:13:47,424
But don't fall into the trap
of the same wishful thinking

280
00:13:47,457 --> 00:13:50,560
and turning away that caused us
to deal with social media.

281
00:13:51,261 --> 00:13:55,298
Your role in this is not
to solve the whole problem.

282
00:13:55,332 --> 00:13:59,436
But your role in this is to be part
of the collective immune system.

283
00:13:59,803 --> 00:14:01,738
That when you hear this wishful thinking

284
00:14:01,771 --> 00:14:03,173
or the logic of inevitability

285
00:14:03,206 --> 00:14:04,407
and fatalism,

286
00:14:04,441 --> 00:14:07,010
to say that this is not inevitable,

287
00:14:07,010 --> 00:14:08,879
and the best qualities of human nature

288
00:14:08,879 --> 00:14:11,081
is when we step up and make a choice

289
00:14:11,114 --> 00:14:13,116
about the future that we actually want

290
00:14:13,149 --> 00:14:15,252
for the people and the world that we love.

291
00:14:15,886 --> 00:14:17,954
There is no definition of wisdom,

292
00:14:17,988 --> 00:14:19,656
in any tradition,

293
00:14:19,656 --> 00:14:22,292
that does not involve restraint.

294
00:14:22,926 --> 00:14:27,163
Restraint is the central feature
of what it means to be wise.

295
00:14:27,163 --> 00:14:30,800
And AI is humanity's ultimate test

296
00:14:30,834 --> 00:14:34,971
and greatest invitation
to step into our technological maturity.

297
00:14:35,505 --> 00:14:39,142
There is no room
of adults working secretly

298
00:14:39,175 --> 00:14:41,378
to make sure that this turns out OK.

299
00:14:42,279 --> 00:14:44,281
We are the adults.

300
00:14:44,281 --> 00:14:45,448
We have to be.

301
00:14:45,849 --> 00:14:48,685
And I believe another choice
is possible with AI

302
00:14:48,685 --> 00:14:51,354
if we can commonly recognize
what we have to do.

303
00:14:52,222 --> 00:14:54,457
And eight years from now,

304
00:14:54,491 --> 00:14:57,127
I'd like to come back to this stage,

305
00:14:57,160 --> 00:14:59,963
not to talk about more
problems with technology,

306
00:14:59,996 --> 00:15:02,832
but to celebrate how we stepped up

307
00:15:02,832 --> 00:15:04,434
and solved this one.

308
00:15:05,135 --> 00:15:06,336
Thank you.

309
00:15:06,336 --> 00:15:13,176
(Applause and cheers)

